# The Dockerfile copies explicit paths rather than the tree, so this file is about the
# size of the context BuildKit uploads, not about correctness. `node_modules` in a pnpm
# workspace is a forest of symlinks into `.pnpm`; sending it costs a gigabyte and is never
# read, because every stage runs its own `pnpm install`.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output. The image builds its own; a stale local `dist` must never win.
dist
**/dist
.next
**/.next
coverage
**/coverage
*.tsbuildinfo

.git
.github
.superpowers
.vercel
docs
# The load-test harness runs against a built image from outside it, so it is context the
# build uploads and no stage ever copies.
infra

# Never into an image, and this repository is public so anything that lands there is
# rotated rather than deleted.
#
# BOTH FORMS, and the recursive ones are the ones that matter (F-317). Docker matches
# these against the context-relative path, so a pattern with no `**/` prefix matches at
# the context root ONLY — which is why this file already carries `dist` and `**/dist`.
# The secret block had only the root form, so `apps/web/.env.local`, which
# `apps/web/.env.example` line 1 tells developers to create and which holds
# `BFF_PROXY_SECRET`, was uploaded into every build context.
#
# Reading `.gitignore` is what makes that gap invisible: its patterns ARE recursive, so
# the file is correctly never committed, and a reader assumes the block two directories
# away does the same job.
#
# This is the backstop. The guarantee is that both Dockerfiles COPY an explicit list and
# neither writes `COPY apps/web apps/web` (ADR-0035).
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
.npmrc
**/.npmrc
*.pem
**/*.pem
*.key
**/*.key

Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.test.yml
README.md
